                          "carry an umbrella"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "likelihood",
                    "title": "Talking about likelihood",
                    "explanation": "Estructuras para hablar de probabilidad y de lo previsto, muy habituales en entrevistas y noticias:\n- «be bound to»: es casi seguro: «I'm bound to spend a lot of time on planes.»\n- «be (un)likely to» / «it's (un)likely that»: probable / poco probable: «I'm unlikely to move permanently.»\n- «be set to»: está previsto (muy típico de la prensa): «We're set to hire four designers.»\n- «be due to»: está programado para una fecha: «The office is due to open in October.»\n- «be on the verge» / «brink of» + «-ing»: estar a punto de (algo importante).\n- «stand a (good) chance (of» + «-ing)»: tener (buenas) posibilidades.\n- «be about to»: estar a punto de (inminente).",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Expresión",
                        "Significado",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "be bound to",
                          "es casi seguro",
                          "I'm bound to spend a lot of time on planes."
                        ],
                        [
                          "be likely / unlikely to",
                          "probable / poco probable",
                          "I'm unlikely to move permanently."
                        ],
                        [
                          "be set to",
                          "está previsto",
                          "We're set to hire four designers."
                        ],
                        [
                          "be due to",
                          "está programado",
                          "The office is due to open in October."
                        ],
                        [
                          "be on the verge of + -ing",
                          "a punto de",
                          "on the verge of disappearing"
                        ],
                        [
                          "stand a good chance (of)",
                          "tener buenas posibilidades",
                          "We stand a fairly good chance."
                        ],
                        [
                          "be about to",
                          "estar a punto de",
                          "You're about to open an office in Madrid."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "The Madrid office is due to open in October.",
                      "We're set to hire four designers there by the end of the year.",
                      "I'm unlikely to move permanently.",
                      "We stand a fairly good chance, I'm told."
                    ]
                  }
                },
